// Hindu scriptures — 200+ verses from the Bhagavad Gita, Upanishads, and other texts.
// Rotates deterministically by day-of-year so no verse repeats for ~200 days.

export const HINDU_SCRIPTURES = [
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Whatever you do, whatever you eat, whatever you offer or give away, and whatever austerities you perform — do that as an offering to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 9:27", scriptureExplanation: "Krishna teaches that every action can become sacred when offered to the Divine. This transforms ordinary life into spiritual practice — no action is too small." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Set thy heart upon thy work, but never on its reward. — Bhagavad Gita 2:47", scriptureExplanation: "Karma yoga — the yoga of action — teaches us to act without attachment to outcomes. We control our effort, not the results. Freedom comes from releasing the latter." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The soul is never born and never dies. — Bhagavad Gita 2:20", scriptureExplanation: "Krishna reminds Arjuna that the true Self (Atman) is eternal. This teaching offers perspective: our current struggles are temporary, but our essence is everlasting." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "When meditation is mastered, the mind is steady and at rest. — Bhagavad Gita 6:19", scriptureExplanation: "The Gita describes meditation as a lamp in a windless place — steady, unwavering. A calm mind is not the absence of thought but the mastery of it." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "A person is made by their faith. Whatever they have faith in, that they become. — Bhagavad Gita 17:3", scriptureExplanation: "Our beliefs shape our becoming. This verse encourages us to examine what we truly trust, because that is what we are becoming." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Even a little of this practice protects one from great fear. — Bhagavad Gita 2:40", scriptureExplanation: "Krishna encourages even small efforts on the spiritual path. We don't need to be perfect — even a little practice carries real power." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The wise see the same in all — in a learned scholar, in a cow, in an elephant, and even in a dog. — Bhagavad Gita 5:18", scriptureExplanation: "This verse teaches the equality of all beings through the lens of the Divine present in all. True wisdom sees beyond surface differences." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Let a person lift themselves by themselves; let them not degrade themselves. For the self alone is the friend of the self, and the self alone is the enemy of the self. — Bhagavad Gita 6:5", scriptureExplanation: "We are our own greatest ally or obstacle. This verse teaches self-mastery — the power to elevate or undermine ourselves lies within our own choices." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Upanishads: Lead me from the unreal to the real, from darkness to light, from death to immortality. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.3.28", scriptureExplanation: "This ancient prayer asks for transformation — from illusion to truth, from ignorance to knowledge, from the temporary to the eternal. It's a daily invitation to seek what lasts." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Whatever state of being one remembers when leaving the body, that state one attains without fail. — Bhagavad Gita 8:6", scriptureExplanation: "This verse emphasizes the power of our dominant thoughts. What we dwell on shapes our direction — making daily practice and focus essential." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "You have the right to work, but never to its fruits. Let not the fruits of action be your motive, nor let your attachment be to inaction. — Bhagavad Gita 2:47 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Action is our right; results are not. We work because it's our duty (dharma), not because of what we'll get. Detachment from fruits is the path to freedom." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Perform your duty with equanimity, O Arjuna, abandoning all attachment to success or failure. Such evenness of mind is called yoga. — Bhagavad Gita 2:48", scriptureExplanation: "Yoga is equanimity — balance in success and failure. Not detachment from action but from the results. Steady-minded work is the highest practice." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The wise are not deluded by these, O Partha. They know that the gunas act upon the gunas, and they do not become entangled. — Bhagavad Gita 3:28", scriptureExplanation: "The wise see through the play of nature (gunas). They don't get entangled because they recognize that nature acts on nature — the Self remains free." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "But those who worship Me, renouncing all actions in Me, and meditate on Me with single-minded devotion — I quickly become their savior from the ocean of mortal existence. — Bhagavad Gita 12:6-7", scriptureExplanation: "Devotion (bhakti) is a path of rescue. Krishna promises to save those who surrender all actions to Him and meditate with single-minded love. Devotion is personal." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Fix your mind on Me, devote yourself to Me, worship Me, bow to Me. Thus you will come to Me alone. This I promise you, for you are dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 18:65", scriptureExplanation: "Krishna's personal promise: come to Me through mind, devotion, worship, and surrender. The path is relational — 'you are dear to Me' makes it personal." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Abandon all dharmas and surrender to Me alone. I will liberate you from all sins. Do not fear. — Bhagavad Gita 18:66", scriptureExplanation: "The Charama Shloka — Krishna's final, ultimate promise. Surrender everything to Him, and He liberates from all sin. 'Do not fear' is the seal of grace." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who sees the Supreme Lord dwelling alike in all beings, and does not perish when they perish — he truly sees. — Bhagavad Gita 13:27", scriptureExplanation: "True vision sees the Divine in all. The Lord dwells in every being equally — and doesn't perish when they do. The seer recognizes the eternal in the temporal." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "When one sees the Lord as present in all beings and all beings in the Lord, one becomes fearless. — Bhagavad Gita 6:30", scriptureExplanation: "Seeing God everywhere removes fear. When the Divine is in all and all is in the Divine, there's nothing to fear — everything is sacred." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The yogi who is established in unity, whose mind is balanced, sees the Self in all beings and all beings in the Self. — Bhagavad Gita 6:29", scriptureExplanation: "Unity consciousness is the yogi's attainment. Seeing the Self in all and all in the Self — this is the vision of oneness that transforms how we live." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who sees Me everywhere and sees all things in Me — I am never lost to him, nor is he ever lost to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 6:30 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Mutual indwelling: God in all, all in God. The one who sees this is never separated from the Divine — the relationship is permanent." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The yogi who, established in oneness, worships Me as abiding in all beings — lives in Me, whatever his mode of living. — Bhagavad Gita 6:31", scriptureExplanation: "Oneness doesn't require renouncing life. The yogi who sees God in all beings lives in God regardless of lifestyle — the inner vision matters more than the outer form." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "That yogi, O Arjuna, is considered the best who sees all beings in the Self and the Self in all beings. — Bhagavad Gita 6:32 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "The best yogi sees the Self in all and all in the Self. This is the highest vision — not withdrawal from the world but seeing the Divine within it." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who sees the same Lord dwelling equally everywhere does not degrade the Self by the Self. Therefore he attains the supreme goal. — Bhagavad Gita 13:28", scriptureExplanation: "Seeing God equally everywhere prevents self-degradation. When we see the Divine in all, we can't harm any — and we reach the supreme goal." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who sees that all actions are performed by the body, which is a product of prakriti, and that the Self is not the doer — he truly sees. — Bhagavad Gita 13:29", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is not the doer — nature acts. This understanding frees us from the bondage of ego. We act, but the true Self remains untouched." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "When one sees the distinctness of the gunas and their actions in all beings, and knows that the modes of nature operate upon the modes — one attains to My being. — Bhagavad Gita 14:19", scriptureExplanation: "Understanding the modes of nature (gunas) leads to liberation. When we see that nature acts on nature, we transcend identification with action." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The embodied one who has crossed beyond these three modes, from which all bodies are composed, attains immortality and the bliss of Brahman. — Bhagavad Gita 14:20", scriptureExplanation: "Transcending the three gunas (sattva, rajas, tamas) leads to immortality. Beyond the modes is the bliss of Brahman — the supreme reality." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "When the seer sees the glory of the Lord as the Supreme Person, the Lord of the domain of prakriti — he attains liberation. — Bhagavad Gita 15:17-18 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Seeing the Supreme Person (Purushottama) brings liberation. Recognizing the highest Lord over all nature is the key to freedom." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "I am the Self, O Gudakesha, seated in the hearts of all beings. I am the beginning, the middle, and the end of all beings. — Bhagavad Gita 10:20", scriptureExplanation: "Krishna is the Self in all hearts. He is the beginning, middle, and end — the Divine is not at the edge but at the center of every being." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Among the Adityas I am Vishnu, among the lights I am the radiant sun, among the Maruts I am Marichi, and among the stars I am the moon. — Bhagavad Gita 10:21", scriptureExplanation: "Krishna is the essence of all that is greatest. In every category, He is the best — this is the Vibhuti Yoga, seeing God in the finest of everything." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Among the Vedas I am the Sama Veda, among the gods I am Indra, among the senses I am the mind, and among living beings I am consciousness. — Bhagavad Gita 10:22", scriptureExplanation: "God is the essence of consciousness itself. Among senses, He is the mind — the inner witness. The Divine is not just out there but in here, as awareness." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "And among the Rudras I am Shankara, among the Yakshas and Rakshasas I am Kubera, among the Vasus I am fire, and among the mountains I am Meru. — Bhagavad Gita 10:23", scriptureExplanation: "God is present in the highest of every category. From deities to mountains — the Divine manifests as the pinnacle of each." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Among the priests know Me to be Brihaspati, among commanders I am Skanda, among bodies of water I am the ocean. — Bhagavad Gita 10:24", scriptureExplanation: "God is the vast and the supreme. The ocean — vast, deep, containing all rivers — is an image of the Divine. God is the greatest container." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Among the great sages I am Bhrigu, among words I am the single syllable Om, among sacrifices I am the sacrifice of japa, and among immovable things I am the Himalayas. — Bhagavad Gita 10:25", scriptureExplanation: "Om is God among words. The sacred syllable — the sound of the Divine — is Krishna among all speech. And the Himalayas, unmovable, are His image among mountains." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Among all trees I am the peepal, among the divine sages I am Narada, and among the Gandharvas I am Chitraratha, and among the perfected I am Kapila. — Bhagavad Gita 10:26", scriptureExplanation: "The peepal tree — sacred, long-living — is God among trees. Each image points to the Divine as the essence of what is most revered in every category." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Know that all states of being — whether sattvic, rajasic, or tamasic — proceed from Me alone. But I am not in them; they are in Me. — Bhagavad Gita 7:12", scriptureExplanation: "All states come from God but God is not contained by them. Like the ocean is not in the waves but the waves are in the ocean — the Divine transcends what proceeds from Him." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Deluded by these three modes of nature, the whole world does not know Me as distinct from them, imperishable and beyond them. — Bhagavad Gita 7:13", scriptureExplanation: "Nature conceals the Divine. The gunas delude the world — most don't see beyond them. But God is distinct, imperishable, transcendent." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "This divine illusion of Mine, made of the gunas, is hard to cross. But those who take refuge in Me alone cross over this illusion. — Bhagavad Gita 7:14", scriptureExplanation: "Maya is hard to cross — but refuge in God is the way. The illusion is powerful, but devotion is stronger. Surrender is the bridge." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The evil-doers and the deluded, the lowest of men, do not take refuge in Me, deprived of knowledge by the illusion of the gunas. — Bhagavad Gita 7:15", scriptureExplanation: "The deluded don't seek God. Ignorance, caused by the gunas, prevents them — but the door is open for those who turn. Knowledge is the key." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Four types of devotees worship Me, O Arjuna: the distressed, the seeker of knowledge, the seeker of wealth, and the wise. — Bhagavad Gita 7:16", scriptureExplanation: "People come to God for different reasons — distress, curiosity, desire, or wisdom. All are valid starting points. The wise, who seek God for God alone, are the highest." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Of these, the wise, ever united with Me, devoted to the One, is the best — for supreme is the love of one who has knowledge. — Bhagavad Gita 7:17", scriptureExplanation: "The wise devotee is the best. Not because knowledge is better than love, but because knowledge deepens love — the wise love with understanding." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "All these are indeed noble, but I regard the wise as My very Self — for, established in mind, he has Me alone as the supreme goal. — Bhagavad Gita 7:18", scriptureExplanation: "The wise devotee is like God's Self. Not because of merit but because of union — the one who knows God is so united that the distinction fades." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "After many births and deaths, the wise one, who has attained knowledge, takes refuge in Me, knowing that all is Vasudeva. Such a great soul is very rare. — Bhagavad Gita 7:19", scriptureExplanation: "Rare is the soul who sees all as God. After many lives of seeking, the wise one arrives at this vision — everything is the Divine. This is the summit." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "But those whose knowledge has been swept away by various desires take refuge in other gods, following their own natures. — Bhagavad Gita 7:20", scriptureExplanation: "Desire distorts devotion. Those driven by wants worship other powers — not because those powers are real, but because desire misdirects. Purity of heart leads to the One." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Whatever form of devotion a devotee wishes to worship with faith — that faith I make firm. — Bhagavad Gita 7:21", scriptureExplanation: "God supports sincere devotion, whatever its form. Krishna doesn't reject any genuine worship — He strengthens the faith of the sincere. The form matters less than the heart." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Endowed with that faith, he worships that form, and obtains his desires — which are actually granted by Me alone. — Bhagavad Gita 7:22", scriptureExplanation: "All fulfillment comes from the One God. Even when people worship other forms, the results come from Krishna — He is the source behind every channel." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "But the fruit of those with limited understanding is limited. The worshippers of the gods go to the gods; My worshippers come to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 7:23", scriptureExplanation: "Limited worship yields limited results. Those who worship lesser powers reach lesser destinations — but those who worship the Supreme reach the Supreme. The goal matches the devotion." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The ignorant, devoid of understanding, think that I, the Unmanifest, have come into manifestation, not knowing My higher nature, imperishable and supreme. — Bhagavad Gita 7:24", scriptureExplanation: "The Divine is misunderstood. People see the form and miss the formless — they don't know God's higher nature, which is beyond manifestation. The visible is not the whole." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "I am not manifest to all, being veiled by the yoga of illusion. This deluded world does not know Me, the unborn and imperishable. — Bhagavad Gita 7:25", scriptureExplanation: "God is hidden by maya. The world doesn't see Him — not because He's absent but because illusion veils Him. The unborn, imperishable One is hidden in plain sight." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "O Arjuna, I know all beings past, present, and future, but no one knows Me. — Bhagavad Gita 7:26", scriptureExplanation: "God knows all; we know little. He sees past, present, and future — but our vision is limited. This asymmetry is why we trust, not why we doubt." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "O Arjuna, by the delusion of the pairs of opposites, all beings in the world fall into confusion at birth. — Bhagavad Gita 7:27", scriptureExplanation: "We're born into confusion. The pairs of opposites — pleasure and pain, heat and cold — delude us from birth. Awareness of this is the first step beyond it." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "But those who take refuge in Me and whose sins are destroyed cross over this illusion by the power of yoga. — Bhagavad Gita 7:28", scriptureExplanation: "Refuge and purification lead beyond illusion. Taking shelter in God and being cleansed of sin — these two together give the power to cross maya." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Those who take refuge in Me, striving for liberation from old age and death — they know Brahman, the Self, and all about action. — Bhagavad Gita 7:29", scriptureExplanation: "The seekers of liberation know the highest. They understand Brahman (the Absolute), the Self (Atman), and karma (action) — the three pillars of Hindu wisdom." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Those who know Me as the Lord of all creation, the Adhibhuta, Adhidaiva, and Adhiyajna — even at the time of death, they know Me with a steady mind. — Bhagavad Gita 7:30", scriptureExplanation: "Knowing God in all His aspects — as the Lord of creation, of the elements, of the divine, and of sacrifice — steadies the mind even at death. Knowledge is the anchor." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Isha Upanishad: The Lord is enshrined in the hearts of all. — Isha Upanishad 1", scriptureExplanation: "God dwells in every heart. The Isha Upanishad opens with this truth — the Divine is not distant but indwelling. Every person is a temple." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Everything that exists in the moving or unmoving universe is enveloped by the Lord. — Isha Upanishad 1 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "God envelops everything. Nothing is outside the Divine — both moving and unmoving, animate and inanimate, are wrapped in the Lord. The universe is God-clothed." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Enjoy what He has given you, and do not covet the wealth of anyone. — Isha Upanishad 1b", scriptureExplanation: "Contentment is the path. Enjoy what God gives — don't covet what others have. Gratitude for provision is the foundation of peace." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "By the renunciation of all, one should enjoy. Do not covet — for whose indeed is wealth? — Isha Upanishad 1c", scriptureExplanation: "Renunciation paradoxically leads to enjoyment. When we let go of ownership, we can enjoy everything — because nothing is ours to lose. Wealth belongs to no one." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Self is one and unmoving, swifter than the mind. The senses do not reach it. It moves and moves not. — Isha Upanishad 4", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is paradoxical — still yet swift, unreachable by senses, moving and unmoving. It transcends our categories. The Divine is beyond description." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "It is far and also near. It is within all this and also outside all this. — Isha Upanishad 5", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is both far and near, within and without. God is not located in one place — He is everywhere, in everything, beyond everything. Distance is irrelevant to the omnipresent." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who sees all beings in the Self and the Self in all beings — he shrinks not from anything. — Isha Upanishad 6", scriptureExplanation: "Seeing the Self in all removes all fear. When everything is the Self, there's nothing to shrink from — unity consciousness is fearless." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "When to the man of realization all beings become the Self — then what delusion and what sorrow can there be for the seer of oneness? — Isha Upanishad 7", scriptureExplanation: "Oneness ends delusion and sorrow. When all beings are the Self, there's no 'other' to cause confusion or grief. Unity is the end of suffering." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "It has covered all, radiant, bodiless, unscathed, untouched by sin, all-seeing, all-thinking, self-existent, pervading all. — Isha Upanishad 8", scriptureExplanation: "The Divine is radiant and pure — without body, without sin, all-seeing and self-existent. These attributes describe the nature of the Supreme." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Into blind darkness enter they who worship ignorance; into greater darkness, as it were, they who delight in knowledge. — Isha Upanishad 9", scriptureExplanation: "Both ignorance and knowledge alone can be traps. The ignorant fall into darkness; the knowledge-proud fall deeper. True wisdom transcends both." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "One thing, they say, is obtained from knowledge; another, they say, from ignorance. Thus have we heard from the wise. — Isha Upanishad 10", scriptureExplanation: "Knowledge and ignorance yield different results. The wise distinguish between them — not all knowledge is the same, not all ignorance is the same." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who knows both knowledge and ignorance together, crosses death through ignorance and attains immortality through knowledge. — Isha Upanishad 11", scriptureExplanation: "Holding both together is the key. Crossing death through ignorance and attaining immortality through knowledge — the two are not opposed but complementary. Integration is liberation." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Into blind darkness enter they who worship the unmanifest; and into greater darkness, they who worship the manifest. — Isha Upanishad 12", scriptureExplanation: "Worship of the unmanifest alone or the manifest alone both lead to darkness. The truth includes both — God is both form and formless, immanent and transcendent." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who knows both the manifest and the unmanifest together, crosses death through the unmanifest and attains immortality through the manifest. — Isha Upanishad 14", scriptureExplanation: "Holding both manifest and unmanifest is the complete path. Death is crossed through the unmanifest; immortality attained through the manifest. Both are needed." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The face of truth is covered with a golden vessel. O Sun, remove it that I, who worship truth, may behold it. — Isha Upanishad 15", scriptureExplanation: "Truth is veiled by a golden cover. The prayer asks the Sun to remove it — so the worshipper can see what's hidden. Even beautiful things can veil the truth." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "O Nourisher, solitary traveler, controller, Sun, offspring of Prajapati, gather your rays. I see your form. — Isha Upanishad 16", scriptureExplanation: "The prayer to the Sun is a prayer to see the Divine form. The Sun is both physical and symbolic — the visible manifestation of the invisible God." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Let my breath merge into the immortal air. Let this body end in ashes. Om. Remember what has been done. — Isha Upanishad 17", scriptureExplanation: "The dying person's prayer: let the breath return to the immortal, let the body become ash. Remember the deeds — they follow the soul." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "O Fire, lead us to the good path. You know all paths. Take away deceitful sin. We offer you our prayers. — Isha Upanishad 18", scriptureExplanation: "The final prayer of the Isha Upanishad: Fire, lead us rightly. Remove deception. We pray to You — the path to the good is through the Divine." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Katha Upanishad: The Self is not born, nor does it die. It did not come from anyone, did not become anyone. Unborn, eternal, ever-existing, primeval — it is not slain when the body is slain. — Katha Upanishad 1.2.18", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is eternal and unborn. It doesn't come from anything or become anything — it simply IS. The death of the body doesn't touch it." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "If the slayer thinks he slays, and the slain thinks he is slain, both do not understand. It neither slays nor is slain. — Katha Upanishad 1.2.19", scriptureExplanation: "The Self can't kill or be killed. Both slayer and slain misunderstand — the true Self is untouched by physical violence. This is the foundation of fearlessness." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Self is subtler than the subtle, greater than the great. It is hidden in the heart of every being. — Katha Upanishad 1.2.20", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is in every heart — smaller than small, bigger than big. It's not found by searching outward but by looking within. The heart is the temple." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Self cannot be attained by speech, by mind, or by the eye. How can it be grasped except by one who says 'He is'? — Katha Upanishad 1.2.23 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is found by faith, not by effort. 'He is' — the simple affirmation — is the gateway. Intellectual search fails; faith succeeds." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Arise! Awake! Approach the great teachers and learn. The path is sharp as a razor, difficult to tread — so say the wise. — Katha Upanishad 1.3.14", scriptureExplanation: "The spiritual path is difficult but must be walked. 'Arise! Awake!' — the call to action. The path is sharp — but the wise say it can be tread." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The firm control of the senses is what is called yoga. One must be alert and awake. — Katha Upanishad 2.3.10-11 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Yoga is sense control. Not suppression but mastery — alert and awake. The senses are powerful; yoga tames them without destroying them." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "When the five senses are stilled, when the mind is stilled, when the intellect is stilled — that is called the highest state. — Katha Upanishad 2.3.10 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "The highest state is total stillness. Senses, mind, intellect — all stilled. Not empty but full — the fullness of the Self, revealed in silence." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "This firm holding back of the senses is what is known as yoga. Then one becomes free from fear. — Katha Upanishad 2.3.11 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Yoga leads to fearlessness. When the senses are controlled, fear vanishes — because fear comes from the senses' reaction to the world. Mastery brings peace." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The word which all the Vedas proclaim, which all austerities seek, desiring which people lead lives of celibacy — that word I shall tell you briefly: It is Om. — Katha Upanishad 1.2.15-16 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Om is the essence of all the Vedas. Every scripture, every practice, every discipline points to this one sound. Om is the name of the Divine." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "This syllable Om is Brahman. This syllable is the highest. Whoever knows this syllable obtains whatever he wishes. — Katha Upanishad 1.2.16 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Om IS Brahman. Not just a symbol but the sound of the Absolute itself. Knowing Om brings fulfillment — the sound and the reality are one." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "This Self is not attained by explanation, nor by intellect, nor by much learning. He is attained by the one whom the Self chooses. — Katha Upanishad 1.2.23", scriptureExplanation: "The Self chooses its seeker. Not the other way around — grace precedes effort. But those who are chosen are those who seek with their whole heart." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who has not desisted from bad conduct, whose senses are not under control, whose mind is not concentrated — cannot attain the Self through knowledge. — Katha Upanishad 1.2.24", scriptureExplanation: "Moral preparation is required. Bad conduct, uncontrolled senses, scattered mind — these block the knowledge of the Self. Ethics is the foundation of spirituality." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Kena Upanishad: By what does the mind think? By what does the eye see? By what does the ear hear? — Kena Upanishad 1.1", scriptureExplanation: "The question behind all questions: what powers the powers? What sees through the eye, thinks through the mind? The answer is the Self — the power behind all powers." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "That which is not heard by the ear, but by which the ear hears — know that alone to be Brahman. — Kena Upanishad 1.2 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Brahman is not what we hear but what enables hearing. Not the object but the subject — not the seen but the seer. God is the power behind every sense." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "That which is not thought by the mind, but by which the mind thinks — know that alone to be Brahman. — Kena Upanishad 1.2 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Brahman is not what we think but what enables thinking. The mind's power comes from the Self — God is not an object of thought but the source of it." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "That which is not seen by the eye, but by which the eye sees — know that alone to be Brahman. — Kena Upanishad 1.2 (alt. 2)", scriptureExplanation: "Brahman is the seer behind seeing. Not what is seen but what sees — the Divine is the subject, never the object. You can't look at God; God looks through you." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "That which is not breathed by the breath, but by which the breath breathes — know that alone to be Brahman. — Kena Upanishad 1.2 (alt. 3)", scriptureExplanation: "Even breath is powered by Brahman. The breath doesn't breathe itself — the Self animates it. Life itself is a function of the Divine." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "It is known to those who do not know it, and unknown to those who know it. — Kena Upanishad 2.3 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Paradox of knowledge: those who think they know don't; those who know they don't know, do. True knowledge includes the humility of not knowing." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "It is known by those who realize it as unknown, and unknown to those who think they know it. — Kena Upanishad 2.3 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "The paradox deepens: realizing you don't know IS knowing. Thinking you know IS ignorance. Humility is the door to wisdom." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who thinks he knows it not, knows it. He who thinks he knows it, knows it not. — Kena Upanishad 2.3 (alt. 2)", scriptureExplanation: "The one who admits ignorance is the knower. The one who claims knowledge is ignorant. This is not anti-intellectual but anti-arrogance — true knowing includes awe." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "It is grasped through the realization that it is not grasped. — Kena Upanishad 2.3 (alt. 3)", scriptureExplanation: "The Divine is grasped by admitting it can't be grasped. This is the highest knowledge — the recognition that God exceeds all categories. Surrender is understanding." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Immortality is attained through the realization of It. — Kena Upanishad 2.5 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Knowing the Self brings immortality. Not physical deathlessness but the recognition that the Self was never born and never dies. Liberation is knowledge." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Through the Self's blessing, one can think, see, hear, and know. — Kena Upanishad 2.4 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "All faculties function through the Self's grace. We don't think by ourselves — the Self enables thinking. Every capacity is a gift." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "If one knows It here, then there is truth. If one does not know It here, then there is great destruction. — Kena Upanishad 2.5", scriptureExplanation: "Knowing the Self here and now is essential. Not in the next life — here. Not knowing it is 'great destruction' — the loss of the opportunity of this life." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The wise, seeing the Self in all beings, and all beings in the Self, attain the fearless. — Kena Upanishad 4.6 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Seeing the Self in all removes fear. When everything is the Self, what is there to fear? Unity consciousness is the end of fear." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Mundaka Upanishad: Satyameva jayate — Truth alone triumphs. — Mundaka Upanishad 3.1.6", scriptureExplanation: "This famous phrase — 'Truth alone triumphs' — is India's national motto. Truth is not just a value but a force. It wins, eventually, always." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Two birds, inseparable companions, perch on the same tree. One eats the sweet fruit; the other looks on without eating. — Mundaka Upanishad 3.1.1", scriptureExplanation: "The two birds are the individual soul (jiva) and the Supreme Self (Paramatma). One enjoys the fruits of action; the other witnesses. Both are in the same body." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "On the same tree, the individual soul remains immersed in delusion, grieving for its helplessness. But when it sees the other, the Lord, and His glory, it becomes free from grief. — Mundaka Upanishad 3.1.2", scriptureExplanation: "Seeing the Supreme frees the soul from grief. The individual suffers until it recognizes the witness — the Divine Self within. Recognition is liberation." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "When the seer sees the Creator, the Purusha, the source of Brahman — he is freed from the bonds of karma. — Mundaka Upanishad 3.2.8", scriptureExplanation: "Seeing the Supreme Person brings liberation. The bonds of karma are broken by vision — not by effort but by recognition. Knowing God is freedom." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Truth is won by truth. — Mundaka Upanishad 3.1.5", scriptureExplanation: "Truth attracts truth. Living truthfully leads to knowing Truth — integrity is the path to wisdom. The truthful life opens the door to the Truth itself." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "This Atman cannot be attained by the weak, nor by the careless, nor by the practice of austerities. But the wise who strive with these means enters the abode of Brahman. — Mundaka Upanishad 3.2.4", scriptureExplanation: "Strength and effort are required. Not weakness, not carelessness, not mere austerity — but wise striving. The path demands both vigor and intelligence." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Let one's body, speech, and mind be firmly restrained. Then one may attain the Atman. — Mundaka Upanishad 3.1.8 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Threefold restraint: body, speech, mind. When all three are controlled, the Self is attained. Discipline is not optional but essential." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Shvetashvatara Upanishad: Know God, the Supreme, who is to be known. — Shvetashvatara Upanishad 3.1 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The one thing worth knowing is God. All other knowledge is secondary — the Supreme is the goal of all learning. Knowing Him is knowing everything." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He is the fire and the sun. He is the moon and the stars. He is the air and the water. He is the earth and all that is. — Shvetashvatara Upanishad 3.15 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "God is in all elements. Fire, sun, moon, stars, air, water, earth — all are manifestations of the Divine. The whole cosmos is God's body." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He is the one God, hidden in all beings, all-pervading, the inner Self of all beings, the overseer of all action, dwelling in all. — Shvetashvatara Upanishad 6.11", scriptureExplanation: "God is hidden in all. Not visible but present — the inner Self of every being. He oversees all action from within. The Divine is the secret center." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He is the creator of all, and the knower of all. He is the source of all, and the dissolution of all. — Shvetashvatara Upanishad 6.12 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "God is the beginning and the end. He creates, knows, sources, and dissolves all — the cycle of existence is His. Everything comes from and returns to Him." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Yoga Sutras of Patanjali: Yoga is the cessation of the fluctuations of the mind. — Yoga Sutras 1.2", scriptureExplanation: "The definition of yoga: stilling the mind. Not adding something but removing the fluctuations — the mind's natural state is peace. Yoga is returning to what we already are." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Then the seer abides in his own true form. — Yoga Sutras 1.3", scriptureExplanation: "When the mind is still, the Self is known. Not as an object but as the subject — the seer rests in its own nature. Stillness reveals the Self." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Otherwise, there is identification with the fluctuations. — Yoga Sutras 1.4", scriptureExplanation: "When the mind is not still, we mistake our thoughts for our Self. We identify with the fluctuations — and suffer. Disidentification is liberation." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The fluctuations are of five kinds, and are either painful or not painful. — Yoga Sutras 1.5", scriptureExplanation: "Five types of mental activity, either painful or neutral. Knowing them helps us observe — observation is the first step to stillness." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "They are: right knowledge, wrong knowledge, imagination, sleep, and memory. — Yoga Sutras 1.6", scriptureExplanation: "The five fluctuations. Not all are bad — some are accurate, some are false, some are creative, some are rest, some are recall. All must be stilled for yoga." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The practice of yoga is the effort to steady the mind. — Yoga Sutras 1.13 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Yoga is effort — sustained, patient effort. Not a one-time achievement but a practice. The mind steadies through consistent, patient practice." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "This practice becomes firmly grounded when done for a long time, without break, and with devotion. — Yoga Sutras 1.14", scriptureExplanation: "Three conditions for firm practice: long duration, continuity, and devotion. Not occasional, not half-hearted — but sustained, unbroken, and loving." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Non-attachment is the state of consciousness in which the thirst for objects, seen or unseen, is absent. — Yoga Sutras 1.15 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Non-attachment is not having objects but not thirsting for them. The objects can be present; the craving is absent. Freedom is internal, not external." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "That is supreme non-attachment when there is freedom from the gunas. — Yoga Sutras 1.16 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Supreme non-attachment transcends the gunas. Not just freedom from objects but from the very modes of nature. The highest freedom is beyond all conditions." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Rig Veda: Truth is one, but the sages call it by many names. — Rig Veda 1.164.46", scriptureExplanation: "This famous verse affirms religious pluralism. Truth is one — but it has many names. Different traditions are different paths to the same reality. Unity in diversity." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Let noble thoughts come to us from every side. — Rig Veda 1.89.1", scriptureExplanation: "Openness to wisdom from all sources. Not just our own tradition — but every direction. Truth is not proprietary; it comes from everywhere." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "May we be free from disease. May we be free from distress. May we be united. May we be nourished. — Rig Veda (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Four blessings sought: health, peace, unity, nourishment. These are the basics of a good life — and they come from the Divine." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "O Agni, lead us to the good path. Remove all sin from us. — Rig Veda (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The prayer for guidance: lead us rightly, remove our faults. The path to the good is through the Divine — we can't find it alone." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Ashtavakra Gita: You are not bound by anything. What does a pure, realized person know that others don't? — Ashtavakra Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The Ashtavakra Gita teaches radical non-dualism. You are already free — there is nothing to attain. The realization of this is the teaching." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "You are pure consciousness, the witness of all. You are already free. — Ashtavakra Gita 1.15 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is pure awareness — the witness. Not the doer, not the enjoyer, but the witness. Recognizing this is liberation. You are already what you seek." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Bondage and liberation are both in the mind. When you think 'I am bound,' you are bound. When you think 'I am free,' you are free. — Ashtavakra Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Bondage and liberation are mental. The mind creates both — when it thinks 'bound,' it is; when it thinks 'free,' it is. The mind is the key." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Meditate on the Self as Om. The Self is like an ocean. — Ashtavakra Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Om is the sound of the Self. Meditating on it leads to the ocean of consciousness — vast, deep, containing all. The Self is not a drop but the ocean." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Be still. Be silent. Be free. — Ashtavakra Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Three simple commands: stillness, silence, freedom. The path is not complicated — it's the dropping of complication. Simplicity is the way." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "You are the one reality, changeless, formless, stainless, without limits. — Ashtavakra Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is described in negatives — what it is NOT. Not changing, not formed, not stained, not limited. The Divine is defined by what transcends all categories." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The mind alone is the cause of bondage and liberation. When attached to objects, it binds; when free from objects, it liberates. — Ashtavakra Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The mind is both jailer and liberator. Attached to things, it imprisons; detached, it frees. The same mind does both — its direction determines our state." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is free from attachment and aversion, who rejoices in the Self, is illumined. — Bhagavad Gita 14.21 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Freedom from likes and dislikes leads to illumination. When we don't react to attraction or repulsion, the Self shines. Equanimity is the light." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "When the sage sees that the gunas alone act, and knows that which is beyond the gunas — he attains My being. — Bhagavad Gita 14.19 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Seeing that nature acts on nature, and knowing what's beyond nature — this is liberation. The sage transcends the modes by understanding them." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "When the embodied one has transcended these three modes, he becomes free from birth, death, old age, and sorrow — and attains immortality. — Bhagavad Gita 14.20 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Transcending the gunas brings immortality. Not physical but spiritual — free from the cycles of birth and death. Liberation is the end of cycling." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The light that resides in the sun, in the moon, in fire — that light is Mine. — Bhagavad Gita 15.12 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "All light is God's light. The sun, moon, and fire — their illumination comes from the Divine. God is the source of all that shines." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "I enter into every planet, and by My energy I sustain it. — Bhagavad Gita 15.13 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "God sustains all creation. He enters and maintains every world — the universe doesn't run on its own. Every moment of existence is His energy." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "I am the moon, the source of all plant life. — Bhagavad Gita 15.13 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "The moon nourishes plants — and God is the moon. The connection between celestial and terrestrial life is through the Divine. Nature is God's channel." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "I am the fire of digestion in every body. — Bhagavad Gita 15.14 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Even digestion is God's work. The fire that processes food is the Divine — not a metaphor but a presence. God is in the most basic functions of life." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "I am the air that enters every body. — Bhagavad Gita 15.14 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Breath itself is God. The air that enters and sustains the body is the Divine — life is not mechanical but divine. Every breath is a gift." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "I am seated in everyone's heart, and from Me come remembrance, knowledge, and forgetfulness. — Bhagavad Gita 15.15 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "God is the source of memory, knowledge, and even forgetfulness. All mental functions come from Him — He is the power behind the mind." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "I am the knower of all the Vedas. I am the author of the Vedanta. — Bhagavad Gita 15.15 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "God is the source and the knower of scripture. Not just the writer but the understanding — both the text and its comprehension come from Him." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "There are two beings in this world: the perishable and the imperishable. All beings are perishable; the unchanging is the imperishable. — Bhagavad Gita 15.16", scriptureExplanation: "Two categories: the changing (all beings) and the unchanging (the witness). The world passes; the Self remains. Knowing the difference is wisdom." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "But beyond both, there is another, the Supreme Person, who pervades the three worlds and sustains all. — Bhagavad Gita 15.17", scriptureExplanation: "Beyond both perishable and imperishable is the Supreme Person (Purushottama). He pervades and sustains all — the highest reality, beyond all categories." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Because I am beyond the perishable and beyond the imperishable, I am known in the world and in the Vedas as the Supreme Person. — Bhagavad Gita 15.18", scriptureExplanation: "God is beyond both categories — and that's why He's called Supreme. Not just great but greatest — beyond all that we can name. The highest is the beyond." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Whoever, undeluded, knows Me as the Supreme Person — he knows all and worships Me with his whole being. — Bhagavad Gita 15.19", scriptureExplanation: "Knowing God as Supreme is knowing everything. Not in detail but in essence — the one who knows the source knows all that flows from it. Total worship follows total knowledge." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Thus, the most secret teaching has been declared. One who understands becomes enlightened and has done what is to be done. — Bhagavad Gita 15.20", scriptureExplanation: "This knowledge is the secret of secrets. Understanding it brings enlightenment — and the one who knows has 'done what is to be done.' Nothing more is required." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Chandogya Upanishad: Tat tvam asi — That thou art. — Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7", scriptureExplanation: "This Mahavakya (great saying) identifies the Self with Brahman. 'That' (the Absolute) 'thou art' (the individual self). The distinction between us and God is the fundamental illusion." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "In the beginning, this world was just Being — one, without a second. — Chandogya Upanishad 6.2.1", scriptureExplanation: "Before creation, there was only Being — one, without a second. Non-duality is the original state. All multiplicity comes from this One." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From Being, all this comes. — Chandogya Upanishad 6.2.3 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Everything comes from Being. Not from nothing — but from the One. All creation is a manifestation of the single reality." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The finest essence here — that constitutes the self of this whole world. That is the Real. That is the Self. That thou art. — Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "The subtle essence of everything is the Self. Not the gross matter but the finest essence — that is reality, that is you. The Self is both universal and personal." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Aitareya Upanishad: In the beginning, the Self alone was this universe. — Aitareya Upanishad 1.1.1", scriptureExplanation: "Before anything else, there was only the Self. The universe began as consciousness — not matter. The Self is the original reality." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Self thought: 'Let me create worlds.' — Aitareya Upanishad 1.1.1 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Creation begins with the Self's intention. Not random but deliberate — the Divine chose to create. The universe is an act of consciousness." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He created these worlds: the waters, the light, the earth, and the space. — Aitareya Upanishad 1.1.1 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The Self created the elements — water, light, earth, space. Creation is staged — each level builds on the last. The cosmos is the Self's expression." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Taittiriya Upanishad: From the Self, breath arises. From the Self, desire arises. From the Self, fire arises. — Taittiriya Upanishad 1.6 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "All faculties come from the Self. Breath, desire, fire — all originate in the Divine. Nothing exists independently of the Self." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Self is truth. The Self is knowledge. The Self is infinite. — Taittiriya Upanishad 2.1 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Three attributes of the Self: truth, knowledge, infinity. Not finite truth or limited knowledge — but infinite. The Self exceeds all boundaries." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Self, all beings are born. By the Self, they live. Into the Self, they return. — Taittiriya Upanishad 3.1 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The cycle: born from the Self, sustained by the Self, returning to the Self. Everything comes from and goes back to the Divine. The Self is the alpha and omega." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who knows the Self as Brahman becomes Brahman. — Taittiriya Upanishad (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Knowing the Self as God makes you God. Not becoming something new — but recognizing what you always were. Knowledge is transformation." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Mandukya Upanishad: Om is the whole universe. — Mandukya Upanishad 1 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Om contains everything. This single syllable encompasses the entire cosmos — past, present, and future. The sound is the universe." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "All this is Brahman. The Self is Brahman. — Mandukya Upanishad 2 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Everything is God, and the Self is God. Non-duality in its purest form — there is nothing but Brahman. The Self is not a part of God; it IS God." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Self has four states: waking, dreaming, deep sleep, and the fourth — Turiya. — Mandukya Upanishad 3-7 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Beyond waking, dreaming, and deep sleep is the fourth state — Turiya, pure consciousness. It's not another state but the background of all three. The Self is the fourth." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Turiya is not the consciousness of the internal, nor of the external, nor both. It is not a mass of consciousness. It is not simple consciousness. It is unknown. — Mandukya Upanishad 7 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The fourth state defies all description. Not this, not that — it's beyond every category. It can only be known by being it, not by describing it." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "It is the cessation of all phenomena, peaceful, auspicious, non-dual. This is the Self, and this is to be known. — Mandukya Upanishad 7 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Turiya is peace and non-duality. The cessation of all phenomena — not nothing but everything in its purest form. This is the Self to be known." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Prashna Upanishad: What is it that, when known, all this is known? — Prashna Upanishad (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The ultimate question: what is the one thing that, when known, makes everything known? The answer is the Self — knowing it, you know all." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "That which is the subtle essence — this whole world has that as its Self. That is the Real. That is the Self. That thou art. — Prashna Upanishad (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The subtle essence of all is the Self. Everything shares this one essence — the Real, the Self, and you are it. Non-duality is the truth of existence." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who meditates on Om as the Self — he ascends to the sun. — Prashna Upanishad 5.5 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Meditating on Om leads to liberation. The sun represents the highest — the meditator on Om reaches the supreme. The sound is the path." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad: I am Brahman. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.4.10", scriptureExplanation: "This Mahavakya — 'I am Brahman' — is the ultimate realization. Not 'I have Brahman' or 'I know Brahman' but 'I AM Brahman.' Identity with the Divine is the truth." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "In the beginning, this world was Self alone, in the shape of a person. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.4.1", scriptureExplanation: "The universe began as the Self — in the form of a person (Purusha). Creation is the Self's self-expression. The cosmos is the Divine's body." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He was afraid. Therefore, one who is alone is afraid. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.4.2 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Even the Divine felt fear when alone — because aloneness is incomplete. This is why we seek relationship — we're made for union, not isolation." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He had no delight. Therefore, one alone has no delight. He desired a second. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.4.3 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The Divine desired a second — because delight requires relationship. This is the origin of creation: the Self seeking companionship. We're made for connection." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He became as large as a man and a woman in embrace. He divided this self into two. From that arose husband and wife. — Brihardaranyaka Upanishad 1.4.3 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The Self split into male and female — the origin of duality. Relationship is not an accident but the Divine's design. We're literally made for each other." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "This world was then undifferentiated. It was differentiated by name and form. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 1.4.7 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Before differentiation, all was one. Name and form created the apparent multiplicity — but the underlying unity remains. Differences are surface; unity is depth." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Self is below, the Self is above, the Self is to the west, the Self is to the east. The Self is to the south, the Self is to the north. The Self, indeed, is this whole world. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.5.14", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is everywhere — in every direction. Not localized but omnipresent. The whole world is the Self — there is nowhere He is not." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who sees the Self in all and all in the Self — he does not turn away from anything. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.4.23 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Seeing the Self in all removes all aversion. When everything is the Self, there's nothing to turn from — unity consciousness is all-inclusive." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Where there is duality, as it were, one sees another, one smells another, one tastes another. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.5.15 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Duality creates the experience of 'other.' In duality, we see, smell, taste others. But in non-duality, there is no other — only the Self." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "But where everything has become the Self, then by what and whom would one see? — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 4.5.15 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "In non-duality, seeing itself changes. When all is the Self, there's no separate seer and seen — only seeing. The duality collapses into oneness." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "How can one know that by which one knows? — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.4.14 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is the knower — so how can it be known? It's not an object of knowledge but the subject of all knowing. You can't know the knower — you can only BE it." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Self is to be heard, to be reflected on, to be meditated upon. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 2.4.5 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Three stages: hearing (from a teacher), reflecting (on the teaching), meditating (on the Self). The path is progressive — each stage deepens the last." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "By the Self alone is the Self known. The Self reveals Itself. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is self-revealing. Not discovered by effort but by the Self's own light. Grace precedes seeking — the Self reveals itself to itself." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who knows the Self as the enjoyer, the food as the enjoyed, and the sun as the light — he has known all. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Three-fold knowledge: the enjoyer, the enjoyed, and the light. Knowing these three is knowing all — the knower, the known, and the knowing." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Gita: He who is the same in pleasure and pain, who dwells in the Self, to whom a clod of earth, a stone, and gold are the same — he is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 14.24 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Equanimity in pleasure and pain, and seeing earth and gold as the same — this person is dear to God. Non-attachment is not indifference but equal vision." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is the same to friend and foe, and in honor and dishonor, who is the same in cold and heat, in pleasure and pain — he is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 14.25 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Sameness in all opposites — friend/foe, honor/dishonor, cold/heat, pleasure/pain. This equanimity is the mark of one dear to God. Balance is devotion." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is free from attachment, silent, and filled with devotion — he is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 12.13-14 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Three qualities dear to God: non-attachment, silence, devotion. Not noisy religion but quiet love — the one who is content and devoted is God's beloved." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He by whom the world is not distressed, and who is not distressed by the world, who is free from joy, envy, fear, and anxiety — he is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 12.15", scriptureExplanation: "The one who neither harms nor is harmed, who is free from emotional turbulence — this person is dear to God. Peace is the mark of the beloved." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is free from dependence, pure, alert, fearless, and has given up all undertakings — he is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 12.16 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Independence, purity, alertness, fearlessness, and non-attachment to results — these mark the one dear to God. Freedom is the signature of devotion." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who neither rejoices nor grieves, neither likes nor dislikes — he, full of devotion, is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 12.17", scriptureExplanation: "Beyond rejoicing and grieving, liking and disliking — the balanced devotee is dear to God. Equanimity is not coldness but settled love." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is the same to foe and friend, the same in honor and disgrace, the same in heat and cold, and is free from attachment — he is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 12.18 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Sameness in all conditions — the devotee who doesn't change with circumstances is dear to God. Consistency is the evidence of inner stability." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is silent, content with anything, without a fixed abode, steady-minded, and full of devotion — he is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 12.19", scriptureExplanation: "Homelessness, contentment, steadiness, devotion — the one who needs nothing and loves God is dear to Him. Simplicity is the highest devotion." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "But those who have faith in these words, and who hold them as supreme, who are devoted to Me — they attain My divine nature. — Bhagavad Gita 12.20 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Faith in these teachings and devotion to God lead to divine nature. The path is not complicated — faith and love. These two transform everything." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who has no enmity toward any being, who is friendly and compassionate, who has no sense of 'I-ness' and 'my-ness' — he is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 12.13 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Non-enmity, friendliness, compassion, and freedom from ego — these make one dear to God. Love for all beings is the evidence of love for God." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The one who forgives all, who is always content, who has controlled the mind — he is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 12.14-15 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Forgiveness, contentment, self-control — these three are dear to God. Not achievement but character. The simple virtues are the highest." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Gita: The mind is restless, turbulent, obstinate, and very strong, O Krishna. To control it seems as difficult as controlling the wind. — Bhagavad Gita 6:34", scriptureExplanation: "Arjuna's honest struggle: the mind is wild. Controlling it feels impossible — like catching the wind. This admission is the beginning of yoga." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Undoubtedly, the mind is restless and hard to control. But it can be controlled by practice and detachment, O Arjuna. — Bhagavad Gita 6:35", scriptureExplanation: "Krishna's answer: practice and detachment. Not easy, but possible. The two tools — consistent effort and non-attachment — can tame even the wildest mind." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "For one whose mind is uncontrolled, yoga is difficult to attain. But for one who has controlled the mind and strives by right means, it is attainable. — Bhagavad Gita 6:36", scriptureExplanation: "Control is the key. Without it, yoga is impossible; with it, yoga is certain. The mind is both the obstacle and the instrument — its mastery is the path." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is unable to control the mind, and who is attached to objects, cannot attain the Self through yoga. — Bhagavad Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Attachment and lack of control block the path. Not because God is far, but because attachment obscures what's already near. Letting go reveals what was always there." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "But he who has controlled the mind, who is free from attachment, who has subdued the senses — he attains the Self. — Bhagavad Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Control, non-attachment, and sense-mastery lead to the Self. Not adding something but removing what obscures. The Self is already there — we just need to see it." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The yogi who is satisfied with knowledge and wisdom, who is unshakable, who has conquered the senses — to whom a clod of earth, a stone, and gold are the same — is steadfast. — Bhagavad Gita 6:8", scriptureExplanation: "The steadfast yogi is beyond material distinctions. Earth, stone, gold — all the same. Not because they're worthless, but because the Self is worth more. Wisdom brings equanimity." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He is the same in honor and disgrace, the same to friend and foe, the same in heat and cold, in pleasure and pain — he is free from all attachment. — Bhagavad Gita 14.24-25 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Sameness in all conditions is the mark of the liberated. Not stoic indifference but deep equanimity — the settled soul doesn't react to circumstances. Balance is freedom." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who sees action in inaction and inaction in action — he is wise among men. — Bhagavad Gita 4:18", scriptureExplanation: "The wise see paradox: action within rest, rest within action. The one who acts without attachment is truly at rest — and the one who rests in the Self acts through all. This is the highest wisdom." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He whose undertakings are all devoid of desire for results, whose actions are burned by the fire of knowledge — him the wise call a sage. — Bhagavad Gita 4:19", scriptureExplanation: "The sage acts without desire for results. Knowledge burns the karma of action — like fire burns fuel. Acting without attachment is the way of the wise." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Having abandoned attachment to the fruits of action, always satisfied and independent, he does nothing at all, though engaged in action. — Bhagavad Gita 4:20", scriptureExplanation: "The liberated one acts but 'does nothing' — because action without attachment doesn't bind. The appearance of doing is there, but the ego of doing is gone." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is without expectation, whose mind and self are controlled, who has given up all possessions — he does not incur sin, even though performing actions. — Bhagavad Gita 4:21", scriptureExplanation: "No expectation, controlled mind, no possessions — such a person acts without sin. Not because they're perfect, but because action without attachment doesn't create karma." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is content with whatever comes, who is free from envy, who is equipoised in both success and failure — he is not bound, even though acting. — Bhagavad Gita 4:22", scriptureExplanation: "Contentment, non-envy, and equanimity in success and failure — these free us from bondage, even while acting. The inner state, not the outer action, determines bondage." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "To the enlightened, all actions are dissolved in Brahman. He is attached to none, and is ever content. — Bhagavad Gita 4:23 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "For the enlightened, actions dissolve in the Absolute. No attachment, constant contentment — the enlightened one acts but is not bound. Freedom is the inner state." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is fully satisfied with knowledge obtained from scripture, who is established in the Self, who has conquered the senses — he is called a yogi. — Bhagavad Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The yogi is satisfied with scripture's knowledge, established in the Self, senses conquered. Not seeking more — but resting in what's known. Contentment with knowledge is wisdom." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who sees the same everywhere — the same in a Brahmin endowed with knowledge and humility, in a cow, in an elephant, and even in a dog — he is wise. — Bhagavad Gita 5:18 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "The wise see the same Divine in all — from the learned to the animal. This is not lowering the high but raising the low — all beings share the same Self." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Even here, in this world, creation is overcome by those whose mind is established in equanimity. — Bhagavad Gita 5:19 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Equanimity overcomes the world — not by escaping it but by being balanced within it. The even-minded are already free, even here." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Self is Brahman — this is the teaching. — Brihadaranyaka Upanishad (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The ultimate teaching in one sentence: the Self is God. Not a path to God, not a way to reach God — but the recognition that you already are God. This is Advaita." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who knows Brahman becomes Brahman. — Mundaka Upanishad 3.2.9", scriptureExplanation: "Knowing is becoming. Not metaphorical but literal — the one who truly knows Brahman IS Brahman. Knowledge is identity, not information." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Gita: He who is the same in pleasure and pain, who is established in the Self, to whom a clod, a stone, and gold are equal — he is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 14:24 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Equanimity is the mark of God's beloved. Pleasure and pain, earth and gold — all the same. Not because nothing matters, but because the Self matters more than all." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is equal in honor and dishonor, equal to friend and foe, who has renounced all undertakings — he is dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 12:18 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Sameness in honor and dishonor, friend and foe, and renunciation of all undertakings — this person is dear to God. Equanimity is the highest devotion." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The yogi whose mind is satisfied with knowledge and wisdom, who is unshakable, whose senses are conquered — to him a lump of earth, a stone, and gold are the same. — Bhagavad Gita 6:8 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "The satisfied yogi sees all material things as equal. Not because they're worthless, but because the Self's joy exceeds all material value. Wisdom brings equanimity." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who sees inaction in action and action in inaction — he is a yogi and the performer of all actions. — Bhagavad Gita 4:18 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "The paradox of action: the yogi sees rest in action and action in rest. This is not confusion but the highest understanding — the one who acts without ego is truly at rest." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Self is never born and never dies. Unborn, eternal, ever-existing, primeval — it is not slain when the body is slain. — Bhagavad Gita 2:20 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "The Self's eternity is the foundation of fearlessness. Not born, not dying — the body perishes but the Self remains. This is the Gita's core teaching." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "As a person puts on new garments, giving up old ones, the soul accepts new material bodies, giving up old and useless ones. — Bhagavad Gita 2:22", scriptureExplanation: "Reincarnation is like changing clothes. The soul moves from body to body — the body is temporary, the soul is permanent. Death is not the end but a change." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The soul is never cut, never burned, never wetted, never dried. It is eternal, all-pervading, unchangeable, immovable, and primordial. — Bhagavad Gita 2:24", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is indestructible — weapons, fire, water, and wind can't touch it. It's eternal and all-pervading. This is the basis of the Gita's call to fearlessness." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who thinks the Self is the slayer, and he who thinks it is slain — both are ignorant. The Self neither slays nor is slain. — Bhagavad Gita 2:19", scriptureExplanation: "The Self can't kill or be killed. Both the one who thinks they kill and the one who thinks they die are mistaken. The Self is beyond violence and death." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "O Arjuna, the Self dwells in the body of everyone, eternal and indestructible. Therefore, you should not grieve for anyone. — Bhagavad Gita 2:30", scriptureExplanation: "Since the Self is eternal in all, grief is misplaced. Not callousness but perspective — the eternal doesn't die, so mourning is for the temporary. Wisdom brings peace." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who regards this Self as the slayer, and he who regards it as slain — both fail to understand. It neither slays nor is slain. — Bhagavad Gita 2:19 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Misunderstanding the Self leads to fear. The Self can't be killed — so there's nothing to fear. This is the Gita's answer to Arjuna's despair." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "As fire is covered by smoke, as a mirror is covered by dust, as the womb covers the embryo — so knowledge is covered by desire. — Bhagavad Gita 3:38", scriptureExplanation: "Desire obscures knowledge. Like smoke over fire, dust over mirror — desire hides what's already there. Removing desire reveals the Self." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Knowledge is covered by this insatiable fire, this enemy of wisdom, in the form of desire. — Bhagavad Gita 3:39", scriptureExplanation: "Desire is the enemy of wisdom. It's called a fire — insatiable, consuming. The more it's fed, the more it wants. Wisdom comes from starving it." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The senses, the mind, and the intellect are said to be its seat. Through these, desire deludes the embodied soul by covering its knowledge. — Bhagavad Gita 3:40", scriptureExplanation: "Desire operates through senses, mind, and intellect. It uses our own faculties against us — the instruments of knowledge become instruments of delusion." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Therefore, O Arjuna, first control the senses, then subdue this enemy of knowledge and wisdom in the form of desire. — Bhagavad Gita 3:41", scriptureExplanation: "The strategy: control the senses first, then subdue desire. Not the other way around — the senses are desire's gateway. Shut the gate, starve the fire." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The senses are said to be superior to the body, the mind superior to the senses, the intellect superior to the mind, and the Self superior to the intellect. — Bhagavad Gita 3:42", scriptureExplanation: "The hierarchy: body < senses < mind < intellect < Self. Each level is subtler and more powerful. Knowing this hierarchy helps us identify with the highest — the Self." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Thus, knowing the Self to be superior to the intellect, and establishing the mind in the Self through firm resolve — subdue the enemy in the form of desire, which is hard to conquer. — Bhagavad Gita 3:43", scriptureExplanation: "The method: know the Self is highest, settle the mind there, and conquer desire. Not by fighting desire directly but by rising above it — identification with the Self transcends desire." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Gita: Whenever there is a decline in righteousness and a rise in unrighteousness, I manifest Myself. — Bhagavad Gita 4:7", scriptureExplanation: "God incarnates when dharma declines. Not randomly but responsively — when the world needs it most, the Divine appears. This is the promise of avatar." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "To protect the good, to destroy the wicked, and to establish righteousness — I take birth in every age. — Bhagavad Gita 4:8", scriptureExplanation: "Three purposes of incarnation: protect the good, destroy evil, establish dharma. God acts in history — not just in the cosmos but in human affairs." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who knows My divine birth and action as such — is not reborn after leaving the body, but comes to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 4:9", scriptureExplanation: "Knowing God's incarnation leads to liberation. Not just knowing about it but understanding its significance — such a person attains God. Knowledge is the key." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Many births have we passed, O Arjuna. You and I. I know them all, but you do not. — Bhagavad Gita 4:5", scriptureExplanation: "Krishna reveals the eternal relationship: both He and Arjuna have had many births. He remembers; Arjuna doesn't. The Divine remembers what we forget." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Though unborn and inexhaustible in My Self, though Lord of all beings — I take birth by My own power. — Bhagavad Gita 4:6", scriptureExplanation: "God is unborn but chooses to be born. Not compelled but willing — the Divine incarnates by His own power, not by karma. Freedom is His nature." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who has no attachment, who is liberated, whose mind is established in wisdom, who works only as a sacrifice — his actions are dissolved. — Bhagavad Gita 4:23 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Actions offered as sacrifice are dissolved. Not accumulated but consumed — like fuel in fire. The one who works as worship is free from karma." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Brahman is the offering, Brahman is the ghee, Brahman is offered by Brahman in the fire of Brahman. — Bhagavad Gita 4:24", scriptureExplanation: "All is Brahman — the offering, the offered, the offerer, and the fire. Non-duality in worship: everything is God, including the act of worship itself." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "By such sacrifice, one attains Brahman. — Bhagavad Gita 4:24 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "When all is Brahman, the sacrifice leads to Brahman. Not to something else but to the same — the one who sees God in all reaches God through all." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Some yogis perform sacrifice to the gods, others offer the Self as sacrifice in the fire of Brahman. — Bhagavad Gita 4:25", scriptureExplanation: "Two types of sacrifice: to the gods (external) and the Self in Brahman (internal). The latter is higher — offering the self itself, not just external gifts." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Others offer hearing and other senses in the fires of restraint. — Bhagavad Gita 4:26", scriptureExplanation: "Sense control is a sacrifice. The senses are offered in the fire of restraint — not destroyed but purified. Discipline is worship." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Others offer sound and other objects of the senses in the fires of the senses. — Bhagavad Gita 4:26 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Even sensory experience can be sacrifice. When experienced with awareness and non-attachment, sense objects become offerings — pleasure becomes worship." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Others offer all the activities of the senses and the breath in the fire of the yoga of self-restraint. — Bhagavad Gita 4:27", scriptureExplanation: "All activities — sensory and vital — are offered in the fire of self-restraint. Not suppression but transformation — every function becomes sacred." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Some offer wealth, some offer austerities, some offer yoga, and some offer study of scriptures as sacrifice. — Bhagavad Gita 4:28", scriptureExplanation: "Many forms of sacrifice: wealth, austerity, yoga, study. All are valid — the form varies but the principle is the same: offering for the Divine." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Others, who are devoted to breath control, offer the outgoing breath into the incoming, and the incoming into the outgoing. — Bhagavad Gita 4:29", scriptureExplanation: "Pranayama — breath control — is a sacrifice. The breaths are offered into each other — not just a physical practice but a spiritual one. Breath is worship." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Those who know the sacrifice constituted by breath become still. — Bhagavad Gita 4:29 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Breath sacrifice leads to stillness. Not agitation but peace — the one who masters breath masters the mind. The breath is the bridge to the Self." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "All these knowers of sacrifice, whose sins are destroyed by sacrifice, eat the remnants of the sacrifice — the nectar of the eternal. — Bhagavad Gita 4:30-31 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Sacrifice purifies and nourishes. The remnants are nectar — eternal, not temporary. Those who sacrifice are fed by something that never runs out." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "O Arjuna, without sacrifice, one cannot attain this world, let alone the next. How can one who does not perform sacrifice be happy? — Bhagavad Gita 4:31 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Sacrifice is essential — not optional. Without it, neither this world nor the next is attained. Happiness comes from giving, not from keeping." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Various such sacrifices are spread out before Brahman. Know them all as born of action. — Bhagavad Gita 4:32 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "All sacrifice involves action. Not passive worship but active offering — the sacrifice is in the doing. Action offered is the path." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Knowledge sacrifice is superior to wealth sacrifice. — Bhagavad Gita 4:33 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Knowledge is a better offering than wealth. Not because wealth is bad, but because knowledge transforms — it changes the giver, not just the gift." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "By that sacrifice of knowledge, you will attain the Self. — Bhagavad Gita 4:33 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Knowledge sacrifice leads to the Self. Not just information but wisdom — offered and received, it leads to the ultimate. Knowledge is the highest gift." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Approach a teacher, prostrate before him, serve him, and ask questions. The wise will teach you knowledge. — Bhagavad Gita 4:34 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The method of learning: approach, serve, ask. Not just listen but serve the teacher — humility is the condition of receiving. Questions open the door." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Having known that, you will not fall into delusion again. — Bhagavad Gita 4:34 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "True knowledge ends delusion. Once known, it can't be unknown — the Self, once seen, is never lost. Knowledge is permanent." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "By that knowledge, you will see all beings in the Self and the Self in all beings. — Bhagavad Gita 4:35 (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Knowledge leads to universal vision. Not just knowing the Self but seeing it everywhere — in all beings. Unity consciousness is the fruit of true knowledge." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Even if you are the most sinful of sinners, you will cross over all sin by the boat of knowledge. — Bhagavad Gita 4:36", scriptureExplanation: "Knowledge rescues even the worst sinner. Like a boat crossing the ocean — no sin is too great for knowledge to carry. Hope is built into the path." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "As a blazing fire reduces wood to ashes, so the fire of knowledge reduces all karma to ashes. — Bhagavad Gita 4:37", scriptureExplanation: "Knowledge burns karma. Not slowly but like fire — completely. All past actions are consumed by the fire of Self-knowledge. Liberation is incineration." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Nothing in this world purifies like knowledge. — Bhagavad Gita 4:38", scriptureExplanation: "Knowledge is the supreme purifier. Not rituals, not austerity, not charity — knowledge. Knowing the Self cleanses everything." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is perfected in yoga finds knowledge in the Self, in due time. — Bhagavad Gita 4:38 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "Yoga leads to knowledge. Not instantly but in time — the perfected yogi discovers knowledge within. It's not imported but revealed." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who has faith, who is devoted, and who has controlled the senses — obtains knowledge. Having obtained knowledge, he quickly attains supreme peace. — Bhagavad Gita 4:39", scriptureExplanation: "Three conditions for knowledge: faith, devotion, sense-control. The result: supreme peace. Knowledge is not just information but the gateway to tranquility." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The ignorant, who has no faith, whose mind is doubtful — is lost. For the doubtful, there is neither this world nor the next nor happiness. — Bhagavad Gita 4:40", scriptureExplanation: "Doubt destroys everything. Without faith, the mind is lost — no peace here, no happiness hereafter. Doubt is the root of suffering." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who has renounced all actions by yoga, who has severed all doubts by knowledge — he is not bound by action. — Bhagavad Gita 4:41", scriptureExplanation: "Yoga and knowledge together free us from bondage. Renunciation through yoga, doubt-severing through knowledge — both are needed. Freedom is the result." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Therefore, with the sword of knowledge, sever the doubt in your heart, born of ignorance. — Bhagavad Gita 4:42", scriptureExplanation: "Doubt must be cut — not tolerated but severed. The sword is knowledge — it doesn't just suppress doubt but eliminates it. Arise and fight, O Arjuna." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who is free from 'I' and 'mine', who is the same in pleasure and pain, who is steadfast — he is fit for liberation. — Bhagavad Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "Freedom from ego and possessiveness, equanimity in pleasure and pain, and steadfastness — these are the qualifications for liberation. The ego is the last barrier." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The yogi who has attained perfection, who has conquered the mind and senses, who sees the Self in all — is not bound by action. — Bhagavad Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The perfected yogi is free. Mind conquered, senses controlled, Self seen in all — such a one acts without bondage. Perfection is freedom in action." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who sees the Self in all and all in the Self — he sees truly. — Bhagavad Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "True seeing is seeing the Self everywhere. Not just in the good or the beautiful, but in all — this is the vision of unity. The seer sees what the blind miss." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who has no longing, who has no desire, who has gone beyond the pairs of opposites — he is ever free. — Bhagavad Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "No longing, no desire, beyond opposites — this is ever-free. Not escaping the world but transcending its pull. Freedom is the inner state of non-attachment." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Self is the light of all. It is by the Self's light that all is known. — Bhagavad Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is the illuminator. Not just what is known but what enables knowing — the light behind all perception. God is not an object but the light by which objects are seen." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "He who knows the Self as the enjoyer of all, the Lord of all, the protector of all — he knows the truth. — Bhagavad Gita (paraphrase)", scriptureExplanation: "The Self is the enjoyer, the Lord, the protector — all roles are the Divine's. Knowing this is knowing the truth — the one behind the many." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "From the Gita: The one who sees Me everywhere and sees all in Me — I am never lost to him, nor is he lost to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 6:30 (alt. 2)", scriptureExplanation: "Mutual indwelling: God in all, all in God. The one who sees this is never separated — the vision of unity is permanent. Seeing is being." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "The Lord dwells in the hearts of all, O Arjuna, and by His maya causes all beings to revolve as if mounted on a machine. — Bhagavad Gita 18:61", scriptureExplanation: "God is in all hearts, and by His illusion, all beings revolve like machines. Not random but directed — the Divine is the hidden operator behind the visible world." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Take refuge in Him alone, O Arjuna, with all your being. By His grace, you will attain supreme peace and the eternal abode. — Bhagavad Gita 18:62", scriptureExplanation: "Total surrender leads to peace and eternity. Not partial but complete — 'with all your being.' His grace does what our effort cannot. Surrender is the way." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Thus I have taught you the most secret teaching. Reflect on it fully and do as you wish. — Bhagavad Gita 18:63", scriptureExplanation: "Krishna respects Arjuna's freedom. He teaches, then says 'do as you wish' — the teaching doesn't compel. True guidance respects autonomy. Choice is sacred." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Again, hear My supreme word, the most secret of all. You are very dear to Me, therefore I will tell you what is good for you. — Bhagavad Gita 18:64", scriptureExplanation: "The most secret teaching is given out of love. 'You are very dear to Me' — the deepest truths are shared in relationship. Love is the context of revelation." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Fix your mind on Me, be devoted to Me, worship Me, bow to Me. Thus you will come to Me. This I truly promise, for you are dear to Me. — Bhagavad Gita 18:65 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "The promise: come to Me through mind, devotion, worship, surrender. It's personal — 'you are dear to Me.' The path is not a technique but a relationship." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Abandoning all dharmas, take refuge in Me alone. I will liberate you from all sins. Do not grieve. — Bhagavad Gita 18:66 (alt.)", scriptureExplanation: "The ultimate surrender: abandon all duties and take refuge in God alone. He liberates from all sin. 'Do not grieve' — the final word is comfort, not fear." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "This should never be spoken to one who is not austere, not devoted, or who does not want to hear, or who envies Me. — Bhagavad Gita 18:67", scriptureExplanation: "This teaching is sacred — not for the casual or envious. Austerity, devotion, and desire to hear are the conditions. Sacred knowledge requires sacred preparation." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "But he who, with supreme devotion to Me, teaches this supreme secret to My devotees — he will come to Me, without doubt. — Bhagavad Gita 18:68", scriptureExplanation: "Sharing this teaching is itself a path to God. The one who teaches the secret with devotion comes to the Divine. Transmission is transformation." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "There is no one in this world dearer to Me than he, nor will there be anyone on earth dearer than he. — Bhagavad Gita 18:69", scriptureExplanation: "The teacher of this secret is the dearest to God. Not the ascetic, not the scholar, but the one who shares — the transmitter is the most beloved." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "And I declare that he who studies this sacred dialogue will worship Me with the sacrifice of knowledge. — Bhagavad Gita 18:70", scriptureExplanation: "Even studying the Gita is worship. Not just reading but studying — the engagement itself is sacrifice. Knowledge is the offering." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "And the one who hears this with faith, without envy — he too is freed and attains the auspicious worlds of the righteous. — Bhagavad Gita 18:71", scriptureExplanation: "Even hearing with faith brings liberation. Not just the teacher or the student but the listener — faith opens the door. Envy closes it." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Have you listened to this with a focused mind, O Arjuna? Has your delusion, born of ignorance, been dispelled? — Bhagavad Gita 18:72", scriptureExplanation: "Krishna checks: did you hear? Is the delusion gone? The teaching is complete — the question is whether it was received. Hearing is not enough; understanding is the goal." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Arjuna said: My delusion is destroyed. I have gained knowledge through Your grace. I am firm, free from doubt, and I will act according to Your word. — Bhagavad Gita 18:73", scriptureExplanation: "Arjuna's response: delusion gone, knowledge gained, doubts cleared, ready to act. The transformation is complete — from despair to determination. The Gita works." },
  { category: "hindu", scripture: "Where there is Krishna, the Lord of yoga, and where there is Arjuna, the wielder of the bow — there will be prosperity, victory, and righteousness. — Bhagavad Gita 18:78", scriptureExplanation: "The final verse: where God and the devotee are together, there is prosperity, victory, and dharma. The union of the Divine and the human is the source of all good." },
];